<!--
  ==================================================================
  ORGANISM — the body-level declaration

  A lamina describes one system.  Nothing inside a lamina can describe
  how it reaches another one, because the topology is not a fact about
  either end: "circulation feeds oxygen delivery" belongs to neither
  the circulatory nor the respiratory file, and putting it in one of
  them would make that lamina un-runnable on its own.  So it lives
  here, one level up, the same way ingestion_manifold.xml holds what
  belongs to no single sensor.

  This file declares two things:

    <laminae>    which laminae make up this body, and WHERE each one
                 sits in the stack
    <channels>   the one-way wires between adjacent laminae, and the
                 gates on each end of them

  ==================================================================
  WHY THERE ARE TWO SETS OF GATES AND NOT ONE VALVE

  A crossing used to be one object with a controller on the sending
  side and a write on the receiving side.  That gave the receiving
  lamina no say: when the valve opened, a number landed in it.

  A channel is a synaptic cleft, not a pipe with a tap.  The sender
  has gates on ITS membrane and the receiver has gates on ITS
  membrane, and a value crosses only when both are open.  When the
  sender is open and the receiver is not, the value sits in the
  channel and ages — a real state the one-valve design could not
  represent, and now visible as `waiting`.

  The consequence worth naming: the sender can no longer force
  anything.  Even a flooding sender is only flooding its own side.

  ==================================================================
  THE TWO KINDS OF GATE

  AUTO gates read the state of the lamina they are ON.  They are the
  relief valve: a protection layer, evaluated FIRST, and not
  switchable off by configuration.  A voltage-gated ion channel opens
  on the cell's own membrane potential; it does not wait to be asked.

  KEY gates read what the OTHER side presents at the channel.  They
  are the lock: a ligand-gated channel opens when the right molecule
  arrives.  `identity` matches the LABEL — the right kind of molecule.
  `weighted_distance` and `cosine` match the SHAPE — the right
  structure.  Both are legitimate keys.

  They are two different entities rather than one entity with a
  setting, because only one of them may be disabled and only one of
  them is allowed to ignore the rest of the configuration.

  ==================================================================
  THE TWELVE RULES, enforced when this file is parsed (organism.py)

  1. A gate is CLOSED until its trigger opens it.  Closed is the
     stable state; a pressure-cooker valve is shut and opens on
     condition, not the other way round.  There is no "start open"
     attribute to write, which is the point.

  2. The SOURCE of a crossing is always a composite, never a bare
     attribute.  A composite is the unit a lamina already reasons
     about — it is what a tag addresses and what carries distribution
     weights — so a channel that reads one is reading something the
     model already says is meaningful together.

  3. The DESTINATION is always an attribute declared INTER_LAMINA.
     Writing into another lamina's SENSOR would forge a measurement;
     writing into its PRELIMINARY would overwrite something it
     computes for itself.  The receiving lamina has to have said "this
     is where somebody else's number attaches" before anybody may
     attach one.

  4. Every trigger declares TWO boundaries, and the shut one must be
     strictly under the open one — close_below/open_above for a
     threshold, release_below/match_threshold for a pattern or a
     shape-matching key.  One boundary chatters: the feed carries
     deliberate Gaussian noise, so a value sitting on it opens and
     shuts the gate every cycle.  Measured, on a signal parked on the
     threshold: 177 transitions in 400 cycles with one boundary, 1
     with the band this file declares.  A pattern trigger measured 148
     flips in 400 the same way, which is why the rule covers both.
     `identity` is exempt: a label matches or it does not, and there
     is no noise for a single boundary to chatter on.

  5. Any parameter something may LEARN must be declared here with a
     clamp on it.  Nothing in v2 learns — the key vector is static and
     drift is measured before it is corrected — but the rule is
     enforced now, while there is one place to enforce it, rather than
     after the count of learnable parameters has grown.

  6. One destination attribute, one channel.  Two channels onto one
     attribute do not arbitrate: both report carrying a value and the
     one declared last silently overwrites the other.  Measured before
     the rule was written — one reported sending 6.64 while the
     attribute held 7.95 from the other.

  7. Only ADJACENT laminae couple: a channel steps exactly one level.
     Each lamina is entitled to talk to its neighbours and to nobody
     else, so a body that wants to skip a level has to say what the
     missing middle is.

  8. At most 4 gates per (lamina, side, direction), so at most 8 on a
     lamina.  A face needing more conditions than that is more than
     one face.

  9. ONE interface composite per lamina, the same one in every
     direction.  From outside, the lamina IS that composite; a lamina
     with two faces is two laminae wearing one id.

 10. <transform> is recognised and refused — the v2 lambda, deferred.
     Refusing beats ignoring: a body carrying a transform nobody
     applies would run, and quietly mean something other than it says.

 11. <permeability_gates>, the v1 schema, is refused with a message
     pointing here.  A v1 body running "almost right" on a two-sided
     container is the hardest class of bug to see.

 12. Both <transmit> and <receive> carry at least one gate.  A receive
     side with no gate is a wire writing straight into the far lamina,
     which is the arrangement this whole design exists to forbid.
  ==================================================================
-->
<organism id="bdt_default" version="2.0">

  <name>Default Body</name>
  <description>
    Circulation and respiration, joined at the one seam the two laminae
    already agree on: oxygen delivery needs a cardiac output, and only
    one of them computes it.
  </description>

  <!-- Where each lamina stands.  The stack is a property of the BODY,
       not of the lamina: the same circulatory lamina could sit at a
       different height in a different organism, so its own file's
       `level` is informational and THIS is what adjacency is judged
       against (rule 7).

       Circulation is 1 and respiration is 2, so "up" is the direction
       cardiac output travels.  Nothing physiological forces that
       ordering — it is a declaration, and changing it is an edit to
       this file rather than to any code. -->
  <laminae>
    <lamina id="lamina_circulatory" level="1"/>
    <lamina id="lamina_respiratory" level="2"/>
  </laminae>

  <channels>

    <!-- ==========================================================
         ch_co_to_resp — the first live seam.

         lamina_respiratory declares CO_ref as INTER_LAMINA and says so
         in its own description; this is the other half of that
         sentence.

         Why the source is pump_state rather than CO: rule 2.  The
         channel reads the composite and <extract> names which of its
         attributes goes on the wire.  Reading the composite is what
         lets the sending gate watch the state of the pump as a whole
         while sending only the one number the far side declared room
         for.

         time_delay is 0 because there is nothing to compensate for:
         the receiver re-derives from a crossing within the same tick
         (measured — see the tick order in organism.py), so a delay
         here would be one somebody chose rather than one the machinery
         imposed.  It sits on the CHANNEL because a delay is a property
         of the wire, not of either end.
         ========================================================== -->
    <channel id="ch_co_to_resp"
             from="lamina_circulatory:pump_state"
             to="lamina_respiratory:CO_ref"
             time_delay="0">

      <description>
        Cardiac output crossing into oxygen delivery. Held shut while
        the pump is unremarkable, because a respiratory model resting
        on its own declared baseline of 5 L/min is not wrong — it is
        only uninformed, and an uninformed model that is stable beats a
        coupled one that oscillates.
      </description>

      <!-- Which attribute of the source composite goes on the wire. -->
      <extract attribute="CO"/>

      <!-- ── The circulatory lamina's own membrane ────────────────
           One gate, and it is an AUTO gate: "my output has left the
           band the far side is assuming" is a judgement about MY OWN
           state, made from MY OWN composite. Nothing outside is
           consulted, which is exactly what makes it automatic.
           ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <transmit>

        <auto_gate id="ta_pump_deviation" refractory_cycles="5">

          <description>
            "Unremarkable" is measured against what the receiver is
            already assuming, and it is two-sided. The first version of
            this gate watched the raw normalised CO and opened above
            0.75, which is a cardiac output of 9.5 L/min: it opened for
            a racing heart and stayed shut for a failing one. Measured,
            holding HR at 35: cardiac output settles at 3.39 L/min, the
            seam never opens, and respiration goes on computing oxygen
            delivery from 5.0 — wrong by 32%, silently, in the
            direction a clinician would care about most. A seam whose
            own description says "while the pump is unremarkable" has
            to treat shock as remarkable.
          </description>

          <!-- watch names the attribute the trigger reads; aggregate
               says how to reduce the composite's normalised vector to
               the one number the thresholds compare against.  attr =
               just the watched attribute; mean and max take the whole
               vector; deviation = the watched attribute's DISTANCE
               from reference.  Units are [0,1] — the normalised space
               — so the same numbers mean the same thing whether the
               attribute is in L/min or mmHg.

               reference is the receiver's OWN declared baseline for
               CO_ref (5.0 L/min) expressed in the sender's normalised
               space: (5.0 - 2) / (12 - 2) = 0.30.  That is the point
               the gate should measure departure from, because it is
               precisely what the far side assumes while nothing is
               crossing.  The gate opens when reality stops matching
               that assumption, either way.

               Neither boundary is a taste. open_above comes from the
               RECEIVER's declared tolerance: target_do2 is 1000 +/-
               250 and DO2 = CaO2 * CO_ref * 10, so at the measured
               CaO2 of 19.80 the CO error that pushes DO2 out of
               tolerance is 250/198.0 = 1.262 L/min, which is 0.126
               normalised. 0.125 is that number, and it means the seam
               opens exactly when staying shut would put the far side's
               own outcome out of range.

               close_below comes from measured noise. CO_norm on the
               live feed has sd 0.0104; feeding the trigger a signal
               parked on open_above, 400 cycles:

                 close_below 0.1249 (one boundary)   177 transitions
                 close_below 0.115  (0.010 band)      91
                 close_below 0.100  (0.025 band)       3
                 close_below 0.090  (0.035 band)       1

               0.09 is 3.4 sd of deadband, and the first width where
               the gate stops arguing with the noise. In CO terms: it
               opens when output leaves 5.0 +/- 1.25 L/min and shuts
               when it returns inside 5.0 +/- 0.9. -->
          <threshold watch="CO" aggregate="deviation" reference="0.30"
                     open_above="0.125" close_below="0.09"/>
        </auto_gate>
      </transmit>

      <!-- ── The respiratory lamina's own membrane ────────────────
           This is the half that did not exist before. Respiration now
           has to agree to take the number, and it holds the terms on
           which it absorbs one.
           ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <receive>

        <key_gate id="rk_co" weight="1.0"
                  rate="0.2"
                  on_close="hold" confidence="0.8">

          <description>
            A receptor for cardiac output specifically. `identity`
            matches the LABEL on the channel — the right kind of
            molecule arriving — which is the honest description of what
            this lamina is checking: it wants a cardiac output, and it
            does not care what shape the pump is in as long as the
            thing offered is one.

            Recognising that the pump is FAILING is a different job,
            and it belongs to whichever lamina owns that judgement.
            Here that is circulation, on the transmit side, which is
            why this receptor stays deliberately simple.
          </description>

          <!-- rate is the EWMA coefficient applied once per cycle of
               the RECEIVING lamina, and it lives on this side because
               how fast to absorb is the receiver's business — the
               sender does not get to set the pace at which it is
               believed.

               Measured, holding the circulation at HR 200 so its own
               loop makes CO swing:

                 rate 1.00   CO_ref 13.20 -> 7.13 -> 14.91 -> 10.62
                 rate 0.20   CO_ref  6.64 -> 6.74 ->  8.37 ->  8.82

               0.2 walks the receiver up to the sender instead of
               handing it the sender's argument with itself.

               weight is how much of the offered DEVIATION is taken:
               received = baseline + weight * (offered - baseline). At
               1.0 the receiver takes the whole thing, which is the
               behaviour measured above. It multiplies the deviation
               rather than the value, because a weight on the value
               would turn a cardiac output of 4.8 into 2.4 L/min — not
               a partial reading but a false one.

               on_close="hold" keeps the last value that crossed,
               stamped with its age, so the far side sees an old number
               labelled old rather than one snapping back to a baseline
               nobody chose. decay_to_baseline is the other option, for
               a seam where a stale coupled value would be worse than
               an uninformed one. -->
          <key metric="identity"/>
        </key_gate>
      </receive>
    </channel>

  </channels>

</organism>
